<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · conquest</p><h1 class="enc-title">Spanish colonization of the Americas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1493">1493</a>–<a href="/years/1898">1898</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Spanish colonization of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> began in 1493 on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a>, after the 1492 voyage of the Genoese mariner <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Christopher Columbus</a> under license from Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isabella-i-of-castile">Isabella I of Castile</a>. The Indigenous population fell by an estimated 80% in the first century and a half, primarily through infectious diseases, while forced labour, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> and forced resettlement were imposed and enslaved Africans were imported. An estimated 1.86 million Spaniards settled in the Americas between 1492 and 1832. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-american-wars-of-independence">Spanish American wars of independence</a> of the early 19th century ended Spanish rule over most of the region, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-rico">Puerto Rico</a> were lost to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 1898.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Spanish expansion overseas began under the Catholic Monarchs, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isabella-i-of-castile">Isabella I of Castile</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-ii-of-aragon">Ferdinand II of Aragon</a>. The conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> on 1 January 1492 completed the Christian Reconquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Columbus</a> left Palos de la Frontera on 3 August 1492. The motives have been summed up as gold, glory and God. Authority for expeditions rested with the monarchy, but after Columbus most were paid for by their leaders and participants, who were soldiers of fortune sharing the spoils in proportion to their stake.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Settlement began on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a> in 1493. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes">Hernán Cortés</a> conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire</a> in 1519 to 1521 with many Indigenous allies, among them Tlaxcala. In 1532 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cajamarca">Cajamarca</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-pizarro">Francisco Pizarro</a> and his Andean auxiliaries captured the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atahualpa">Atahualpa</a>, the first step in a campaign that took decades. Expeditions entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-granada">New Granada</a> between 1537 and 1543 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> in 1541, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> was settled for good in 1580. Towns followed a common plan of central plaza and grid, often over Indigenous temples. Two viceroyalties, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a> from 1535 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> from 1542, answered to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-the-indies">Council of the Indies</a>, founded in 1524.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Spanish immigration was encouraged by the Bourbon dynasty, and most settlers came in the 18th century. The Indigenous population fell by an estimated 80% in the first century and a half, mainly through infectious diseases, while forced labour, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> and forced resettlement were imposed and enslaved Africans were brought to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>. The Laws of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgos">Burgos</a> of 1512 to 1513 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-laws">New Laws of 1542</a> sought to restrain settlers. In the early 19th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-american-wars-of-independence">Spanish American wars of independence</a> ended Spanish rule over most of the region, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-rico">Puerto Rico</a> passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 1898.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Spanish colonization of the Americas” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
